{
  "id": 31101862,
  "title": "SPIN-JEvo: Navigating Protein Fitness Landscapes Through Simulated Evolutionary Jumps",
  "doi": "10.6084/m9.figshare.31101862.v2",
  "version": 2,
  "url_public_html": "https://figshare.com/articles/journal_contribution/SPIN-dvEvo_Exploration_of_vast_functional_sequence_space_by_directed_virtual_evolution_from_a_local_sequence_cluster/31101862",
  "description": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><b>SPIN-JEvo</b> is a few-shot virtual directed evolution framework that combines a LoRA-tuned ESM-2 protein language model with a genetic algorithm to efficiently explore protein sequence space and discover high-activity remote homologs from small, binary-labeled sequence datasets. By decoupling sequence exploration from functional constraint, SPIN-JEvo moves beyond the local search limitations of natural and conventional directed evolution. Starting from functional sequences, it first generates likely nonfunctional variants with ~20% random substitutions and then evolves them toward remote functional homologs using a genetic algorithm. Using only a few labeled sequences, SPIN-JEvo identified remote homologs within minutes for both TadA and CcdA, without requiring structural or target-specific information. </p><p dir=\"ltr\">This figshare entry provides the LoRA adapter weights fine-tuned on TadA and CcdA. </p><p dir=\"ltr\">Source code and datasets are available at <a href=\"https://github.com/chenzh-hash/SPIN-JEvo\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_new\">https://github.com/chenzh-hash/SPIN-JEvo</a>, and the framework can be readily adapted to new protein systems through LoRA fine-tuning.</p>",
